{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>And lest you lift up your eyes to the heavens.</b> There is a distinction between the word השמימה ('heavenward') and the word השמים ('the heavens'), just as we find a distinction between מצרימה ('Egyptward') and מצרים ('Egypt'): for מצרימה is the name of the country itself, while מצרים is all the land of Egypt — and the proof of this is (Numbers 20:15): ״וירדו אבותינו מצרימה״ — 'and our fathers went down to Egyptward,' and afterward, ״ונשב במצרים״ — 'and we dwelt in Egypt.' So too here, with the word השמימה: for when Scripture mentions the word השמים in connection with contemplation, the intent in every place is wherever is seen from the heavens, and all directions are equal in this — and that is what it says here, ״תחת כל השמים״ — 'under all the heavens' — that is, in every place that they are. But when Scripture mentions השמימה, it intends the places known by way of wisdom where the principal fixation of the stars is; and through knowledge of their mighty power and their governance on earth, a person comes to err after them. Therefore it says, ״פן תשא עיניך השמימה וראית״ — 'lest you lift up your eyes heavenward and see' — with the eye of the heart, for this matter is not seen and apprehended by the physical eye, but is known and seen by the heart by way of wisdom. And therefore it is written of Abraham (Genesis 15:5): ״הבט נא השמימה וספור הכוכבים״ — 'Pray look heavenward and count the stars' — for He brought him up to a supernal place above the spheres and said to him: Look below, heavenward, to the place where the stars are gathered, if you are able to count them." "<b>And you see the sun and the moon.</b> These are the two great luminaries; ״ואת הכוכבים״ — 'and the stars' — these are the seven wandering stars; the minimum of כוכבים ('stars') is two, and with the ה (the definite article) they are seven. ״צבא השמים״ — 'the host of the heavens' — these are the twelve constellations; ״כל״ — 'all' — includes the ministering angels. And it says ״ונדחת״ — 'and you be driven' — everyone who errs after them is נדח (driven), like a person whom they push and he falls and dies." "<b>Which HaShem your Elohim has allotted to all the peoples under all the heavens.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): אשר חלק — 'which He allotted' — He gave them as their portion. And one should not understand the word חלק in the sense of 'decreed,' for if so it would be an opening for the nations of the world to say that they are compelled to worship idolatry. And because this word is one in which it is possible to err, therefore the seventy elders needed to alter and add for King Ptolemy: אשר חלק להאיר להם — 'which He allotted to illuminate for them.' Thus they interpreted the word חלק to mean that He gave the luminaries and constellations as their portion if they wish to worship them or to worship HaShem, blessed be He — but He did not compel them thereby. And this has already been explained in the perashah of Bereshit." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> אשר חלק — from the root of חלקות (smoothness/flattery) — this teaches that He flattered them with words in order to preoccupy them from the world. Thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded (Job 12:23): ״משגיא לגוים ויאבדם״ — 'He makes nations great and destroys them' — do not read משגיא but משגה — He leads them astray in order to destroy them. And so they said in tractate Avodah Zarah, chapter Rabbi Yishmael: Rabba bar Yitzhak said to Rav Yehudah, 'But there is idolatry in those places where, when the world needs rain, it appears to them in a dream — kill a man and rain will come!' He said to him, 'If you were dead they would not have told you this thing' — that is, were I dead, you would have spoiled the illumination of this matter. Thus Rav said: What is written, אשר חלק ה׳ אלהיך — 'which HaShem your Elohim has allotted' — teaches that He flattered them with words in order to preoccupy them from the world. And that is what Reish Lakish said (Proverbs 3:34): אם ללצים הוא יליץ — 'with the scornful He is scornful' — if one comes to purify himself, they assist him; if one comes to defile himself, they open for him.'" "<b>And you should be roused</b> to reflect that He did not say פותחין ('they open') as He said concerning the good, מסייעין ('they assist'), but rather פותחין לו ('they open for him') — that is, if one wishes to defile himself and to believe a false faith, he will find the doors open."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:45:35.269830735Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}